  "estimatedMinutes": 1,
  "statement": "If \\(1-y\\) is used as an approximation to the value of \\(\\dfrac1{1+y},\\) \\(|y|\\lt1,\\) the ratio of the error made to the correct value is:",
  "answerChoices": [
    {
      "label": "A",
      "text": "\\(y\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "B",
      "text": "\\(y^2\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "C",
      "text": "\\(\\dfrac1{1+y}\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "D",
      "text": "\\(\\dfrac y{1+y}\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "E",
      "text": "\\(\\dfrac{y^2}{1+y}\\)"
    }
  ],
  "answer": {
    "letter": "b",
    "value": null
  },
  "solutions": [
    {
      "id": "sol.ahsme-1965-p18.live",
      "kind": "proof-sketch",
      "text": "The error is \\[\\frac1{1+y}-(1-y)=\\frac{y^2}{1+y}.\\] Dividing by the correct value \\(\\frac{1}{1+y}\\) gives the ratio \\(y^2.\\)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is B.",
      "hints": [
        "Subtract the approximation \\(1-y\\) from the correct value",
        "Divide that error by \\(\\frac{1}{1+y}\\)"
      ],
